<h1 class="article-title">Multimedia Exhibition on the Port of Liepāja Takes Shape in Liepāja Lighthouse</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>Liepāja Lighthouse will soon welcome visitors in a new form, becoming a unique cultural and tourist attraction. Modern technology will tell the story of the Port of Liepāja and allow visitors to explore its development from the earliest days to the present.</p><p>A multi-layered multimedia exhibition is currently being developed in the lighthouse. Across five floors, projections, sound, lighting and animated historical stories will combine to trace the development of the Port of Liepāja from its beginnings to today.</p><p>The project “Creating a Digital Performance on the History of the Port of Liepāja in Liepāja Lighthouse” aims to open one of the port’s navigational facilities to the public, showcasing its cultural and historical significance and creating an innovative visitor experience in which heritage and digital technology come together.</p><p>“This is a unique and challenging project in Latvia. All multimedia elements will be synchronised and operate as a single show across five floors,” says Jānis Zučiks, Project Manager at ProVision Baltic. “Given the lighthouse’s cultural heritage status, it was challenging to install all the equipment without damaging the building and to ensure that the technology operates year-round in Latvia’s changeable weather.”</p><p>Technologically, this is one of the most complex projects of its kind in Latvia. Up to 250-degree projections will be created inside the lighthouse using 14 projectors, accompanied by music specially composed by Andris Vilcāns, sound and lighting effects operating simultaneously across five floors as one multimedia experience.</p><p>Building on years of research by Liepāja Museum, the creative team has developed five animated multimedia stories. Immersive projections and multi-channel sound will reveal the development of the port and city, their international role in trade and transport, and stories of the courage, enterprise and perseverance of Liepāja’s people. The performances are produced by video artist Māris Kalve and specialists from Kalvestudija.</p><p>“The central idea and guiding theme of the Liepāja Lighthouse exhibition is the human will confronting the forces of nature. The lighthouse symbolises the will to live and to exist,” emphasises Māris Kalve, the concept’s creator, director, screenwriter and video artist. “The overarching purpose of the five stories is to remind visitors of the ever-present and absolutely essential value of humanity.”</p><p>Through the multimedia performances, the lighthouse becomes a symbol of overcoming challenges. As visitors climb its vertical structure, they will travel through different eras, discovering the emergence of the port, shipping and fishing traditions, the development of the city and the lives of its people.</p><p>“There are many lighthouses around the world that people can climb to enjoy breathtaking views. But only one will let visitors see not only the landscape, but time itself. That is Liepāja Lighthouse,” says exhibition director and screenwriter Pauls Ķesteris. “Ascending through the lighthouse, visitors move through centuries, exploring the creation of the port and city, human destinies, maritime and fishing traditions, historical upheavals and visions for the future.”</p><p>Liepāja Lighthouse is an integral part of the port’s navigation system and continues to guide sailors and fishermen. The lighthouse belongs to the Maritime Administration of Latvia and has been placed under the management of the Liepāja SEZ Authority.</p><p>Once the project is complete, the multimedia performance about the port’s history will be managed by Liepāja Museum, which will organise guided visits accompanied by a guide or lighthouse employee. The lighthouse will become a significant new cultural, tourist and heritage destination, telling the story of the port’s past and present while looking to its future.</p><p>The project is implemented by Liepāja City Municipality at a total cost of EUR 387,144.34, with co-financing from the European Maritime, Fisheries and Aquaculture Fund, the state and Liepāja City Municipality. The Liepāja District Partnership also supports the project.</p><p> </p></div>
